 "drivingQuestion": "Why is no oath taken on a minor's claim?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Derived from Exodus 22:6",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.42a.12",
   "daf": "42a",
   "sentenceId": 11,
   "he": "אֵין נִשְׁבָּעִין עַל טַעֲנַת חֵרֵשׁ שׁוֹטֶה וְקָטָן, וְאֵין מַשְׁבִּיעִין אֶת הַקָּטָן. מַאי טַעְמָא? אָמַר קְרָא: ״כִּי יִתֵּן אִישׁ אֶל רֵעֵהוּ כֶּסֶף אוֹ כֵלִים לִשְׁמוֹר״, וְאֵין נְתִינַת קָטָן כְּלוּם.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «One does not take an oath concerning the claim of a deaf-mute, an imbecile, or a minor, and» the court «does not administer an oath to a minor.» The Gemara asks: «What is the reason?» The Gemara answers that in the passage from which the halakhot of admission to part of a claim are derived, «the verse states: “If a man delivers to his neighbor silver or vessels to safeguard”» (Exodus 22:6). The word “man” indicates that the reference is only to adults, and «delivery by a minor is nothing,» i.e., it is not recognized as a halakhically significant act, as a minor is not halakhically competent."
  }
 ],
